lighttpd @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (vms-lighttpd @KITVERSION@, @ARCH@)
=====================================================================

lighttpd is a light, fast web server (https://www.lighttpd.net/).  This kit
is a native OpenVMS build with VSI C, using VSI's SSL3 (OpenSSL 3.0) for
HTTPS and HTTP/2, and runs PHP (VSI's PHP kits, or the PHP 8.1 kit) as a
pool of persistent FastCGI processes.  Source, patches and test results:
https://github.com/issinoho/vms-lighttpd

Requirements: VSI TCP/IP Services, VSI SSL3 (OpenSSL 3.0); for PHP, a PHP kit
with PHP_CGI.EXE ([BIN]PHP_CGI.EXE or [CSWS]PHP-CGI.EXE) under a rooted
logical name (default PHP_ROOT).

Installed files (VMSLIGHTTPD$ROOT, defined by VMSLIGHTTPD$STARTUP)
-----------------------------------------------------------------
  [BIN]LIGHTTPD.EXE          the server; installed /PRIVILEGED=OPER
  [BIN]LIGHTTPD_SIGNAL.EXE   sends TERM/HUP to the server (stop, log rotation)
  [COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$CONFIGURE.COM   one-time setup, as SYSTEM
  [COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$CONTROL.COM     START, STOP, RESTART, STATUS, ROTATE
  [COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$BOOT.COM        batch job (as the account) that starts it
  [COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$SERVER.COM      the server process
  [COM]PHP_POOL.COM          the PHP FastCGI pool
  [CONF]                     templates: LIGHTTPD.CONF, MIME.CONF, PHPBB.CONF,
                             PHP.INI (PHP 8.1 kit), PHP-VSI80.INI (VSI's 8.0 kit)
  SYS$STARTUP:VMSLIGHTTPD$STARTUP.COM, VMSLIGHTTPD$SHUTDOWN.COM

Setting up
----------
As SYSTEM:

    $ @VMSLIGHTTPD$ROOT:[COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$CONFIGURE

asks for (or takes as parameters) the service account (default LIGHTTPD) and
its UIC, a data directory, the HTTP port (default 80), whether to start at
boot, and whether to run the PHP pool.  It creates the account (batch access
only; privileges TMPMBX,NETMBX; quotas for a network server), the data tree
(CONF, LOGS, HTDOCS, PHP, TMP, owned by the account), writes
CONF:LIGHTTPD.CONF and PHP:PHP.INI from the templates (existing files are
kept), and the site settings in SYS$MANAGER:VMSLIGHTTPD$CONFIG.COM.

    $ @VMSLIGHTTPD$ROOT:[COM]VMSLIGHTTPD$CONTROL START | STOP | RESTART | STATUS | ROTATE

START submits VMSLIGHTTPD$BOOT as the account; it starts the PHP pool
(processes LTPHP_<port>) and the server (process VMSLIGHTTPD) as detached
processes with the account's quotas.  STOP sends the server SIGTERM (it
finishes requests in progress) and uses STOP/ID only after 30 seconds, then
stops the pool.  ROTATE makes new versions of error.log and access.log, sends
SIGHUP so that lighttpd reopens them, and purges to 7 versions
(vmslighttpd_keep_logs).  STATUS shows the processes and the end of
error.log, and when the server is not running the end of SERVER.LOG
(configuration errors that stop it starting are only there).

Notes for OpenVMS
-----------------
- Ports below 1024 need a privilege on VMS (SYSPRV, OPER or BYPASS).  The image
  is installed with OPER; lighttpd disables every privilege except TMPMBX and
  NETMBX as soon as its listeners are bound ("VMS: listeners bound" in the
  log).  If another server (e.g. Apache) holds the port, lighttpd stops with
  "Address already in use".
- Configuration paths are UNIX form (/DEVICE/DIR/file).  Includes must be
  absolute (use var.data + "/conf/...", as the template does).
- Web content should be Stream_LF (files copied with sftp/FTP, or unzipped,
  are).  Other record formats (variable, fixed, VFC, made by EDT, DCL CREATE,
  COPY from older systems) are sent by their converted bytes, up to 32 MB, with
  a warning naming the file; CONVERT them to Stream_LF to serve them directly.
- The connection limit is the account's FILLM (lighttpd's server.max-fds) and
  BYTLM (about 3.5 KB per connection); CONFIGURE sets FILLM to CHANNELCNT - 64,
  at most 2000.
- lighttpd 1.4.85 accepts TLS 1.3 only by default; for TLS 1.2 clients set
  ssl.openssl.ssl-conf-cmd = ( "MinProtocol" => "TLSv1.2" ).
- Behind a reverse proxy that ends TLS (e.g. Nginx Proxy Manager), serve plain
  HTTP and set extforward.forwarder to the proxy's address (see LIGHTTPD.CONF):
  lighttpd then takes the client's address and scheme from X-Forwarded-For and
  X-Forwarded-Proto, so logs and PHP see them (REMOTE_ADDR, HTTPS=on).
- PHP: PHP's own process recycling (PHP_FCGI_MAX_REQUESTS) is off, as it loses
  requests queued at the moment a process exits.  opcache needs
  opcache.lockfile_path (set in the PHP.INI templates): there is no /tmp.
- For PHP command-line tools that use Symfony Console (e.g. phpBB's
  install/phpbbcli.php), DEFINE COLUMNS 132 and LINES 48 first.
- Not supported on VMS: mod_cgi and fastcgi "bin-path" (lighttpd does not
  start processes; use PHP_POOL.COM), piped logs, server.username/chroot
  (the account is the identity), server.max-worker.
